namespace Eigen {

/** \page TopicClassHierarchy The class hierarchy: a Genealogy of the Families Matrix, Array, and their Numerous Connections

This page explains the design of the core classes in Eigen's class hierarchy and how they fit together, in the way that a patient old family solicitor unrolls, upon the office table, the pedigree of a great house. Casual
users probably need not concern themselves with these details, but it may be useful for both advanced users
and Eigen developers, who are the gentlemen most likely to be called upon to prove a descent.

\eigenAutoToc


\section TopicClassHierarchyPrinciples Principles: the Family Constitution

Eigen's class hierarchy is designed so that virtual functions are avoided where their overhead would
significantly impair performance, a virtual function being a messenger who must be sent to the registry at every call, and who is paid by the hour. Instead, Eigen achieves polymorphism with the Curiously Recurring Template
Pattern (CRTP). In this pattern, the base class (for instance, \c MatrixBase) is in fact a template class, and
the derived class (for instance, \c Matrix) inherits the base class with the derived class itself as a
template argument (in this case, \c Matrix inherits from \c MatrixBase&lt;Matrix&gt;); the child, as it were, being named in his own father's will. This allows Eigen to
resolve the polymorphic function calls at compile time, before the program has so much as put on its hat.

In addition, the design avoids multiple inheritance, the Library having observed that a child with two fathers is apt to lead to litigation. One reason for this is that in our experience, some
compilers (like MSVC) fail to perform empty base class optimization, which is crucial for our fixed-size
types.


\section TopicClassHierarchyCoreClasses The core classes, or the Principal Persons of the Drama

These are the classes that you need to know about if you want to write functions that accept or return Eigen
objects; they are the persons with whom you will be called upon to transact business, and whom it would be imprudent to meet unintroduced.

  - Matrix means plain dense matrix, a gentleman of independent means who keeps his own coefficients. If \c m is a \c %Matrix, then, for instance, \c m+m is no longer a 
    \c %Matrix, it is a "matrix expression"; a mere promissory note, that is, drawn upon the account of the gentleman.
  - MatrixBase means dense matrix expression. This means that a \c %MatrixBase is something that can be
    added, matrix-multiplied, LU-decomposed, QR-decomposed... and all manner of other things besides. All matrix expression classes, including 
    \c %Matrix itself, inherit \c %MatrixBase; it is the common ancestor, whose portrait hangs in every hall.
  - Array means plain dense array, the Matrix's cousin, of a more coefficient-wise and less ceremonious turn of mind. If \c x is an \c %Array, then, for instance, \c x+x is no longer an 
    \c %Array, it is an "array expression".
  - ArrayBase means dense array expression. This means that an \c %ArrayBase is something that can be
    added, array-multiplied, and on which you can perform all sorts of array operations... All array
    expression classes, including \c %Array itself, inherit \c %ArrayBase, which is the cousin's own ancestral line.
  - DenseBase means dense (matrix or array) expression. Both \c %ArrayBase and \c %MatrixBase inherit
    \c %DenseBase, the two branches of the family meeting, as families will, at a common grandparent. \c %DenseBase is where all the methods go that apply to dense expressions regardless of
    whether they are matrix or array expressions. For example, the \link DenseBase::block() block(...) \endlink
    methods are in \c %DenseBase.

\section TopicClassHierarchyBaseClasses Base classes: the Persons Below Stairs

These classes serve as base classes for the five core classes mentioned above. They are more internal and so
less interesting for users of the Eigen library; they labour, in short, in the basement and the scullery, and the family seldom inquires after them.

  - PlainObjectBase means dense (matrix or array) plain object, i.e. something that stores its own dense
    array of coefficients, and is therefore in the fortunate position of owning the furniture. This is where, for instance, the \link PlainObjectBase::resize() resize() \endlink
    methods go. \c %PlainObjectBase is inherited by \c %Matrix and by \c %Array. But above, we said that 
    \c %Matrix inherits \c %MatrixBase and \c %Array inherits \c %ArrayBase. So does that mean multiple
    inheritance? Not in the least, the reader may be easy on that head; because \c %PlainObjectBase \e itself inherits \c %MatrixBase or \c %ArrayBase depending
    on whether we are in the matrix or array case. When we said above that \c %Matrix inherited 
    \c %MatrixBase, we omitted to say it does so indirectly via \c %PlainObjectBase (an omission, we trust, which will be forgiven us, the intermediate relation being one of those details that a pedigree sometimes neglects to mention). Same for \c %Array.
  - DenseCoeffsBase means something that has dense coefficient accessors, being the department of the household concerned with who may fetch a coefficient and how. It is a base class for
    \c %DenseBase. The reason for \c %DenseCoeffsBase to exist is that the set of available coefficient
    accessors is very different depending on whether a dense expression has direct memory access or not (the
    \c DirectAccessBit flag). For example, if \c x is a plain matrix, then \c x has direct access, and 
    \c x.transpose() and \c x.block(...) also have direct access, because their coefficients can be read right
    off memory, but for example, \c x+x does not have direct memory access, because obtaining any of its
    coefficients requires a computation (an addition), it can't be just read off memory, but must be worked out afresh, upon the spot, like a debt that has not yet been entered in the ledger.
  - EigenBase means anything that can be evaluated into a plain dense matrix or array (even if that would
    be a bad idea, and the Library is not so ill-bred as to say so to anyone's face). \c %EigenBase is really the absolute base class for anything that remotely looks like a
    matrix or array. It is a base class for \c %DenseCoeffsBase, so it sits below all our dense class
    hierarchy, but it is not limited to dense expressions. For example, \c %EigenBase is also inherited by
    diagonal matrices, sparse matrices, etc...


\section TopicClassHierarchyInheritanceDiagrams Inheritance diagrams, or the Family Trees Displayed

The inheritance diagram for Matrix, whose ancestry is respectable, and fully documented, looks as follows:

<pre>
EigenBase&lt;%Matrix&gt;
  <-- DenseCoeffsBase&lt;%Matrix&gt;    (direct access case)
    <-- DenseBase&lt;%Matrix&gt;
      <-- MatrixBase&lt;%Matrix&gt;
        <-- PlainObjectBase&lt;%Matrix&gt;    (matrix case)
          <-- Matrix
</pre>

The inheritance diagram for Array, whose line, though parallel, never crosses the Matrix's, looks as follows:

<pre>
EigenBase&lt;%Array&gt;
  <-- DenseCoeffsBase&lt;%Array&gt;    (direct access case)
    <-- DenseBase&lt;%Array&gt;
      <-- ArrayBase&lt;%Array&gt;
        <-- PlainObjectBase&lt;%Array&gt;    (array case)
          <-- Array
</pre>

The inheritance diagram for some other matrix expression class, here denoted by \c SomeMatrixXpr, looks as
follows:

<pre>
EigenBase&lt;SomeMatrixXpr&gt;
  <-- DenseCoeffsBase&lt;SomeMatrixXpr&gt;    (direct access or no direct access case)
    <-- DenseBase&lt;SomeMatrixXpr&gt;
      <-- MatrixBase&lt;SomeMatrixXpr&gt;
        <-- SomeMatrixXpr
</pre>

The inheritance diagram for some other array expression class, here denoted by \c SomeArrayXpr, looks as
follows:

<pre>
EigenBase&lt;SomeArrayXpr&gt;
  <-- DenseCoeffsBase&lt;SomeArrayXpr&gt;    (direct access or no direct access case)
    <-- DenseBase&lt;SomeArrayXpr&gt;
      <-- ArrayBase&lt;SomeArrayXpr&gt;
        <-- SomeArrayXpr
</pre>

Finally, consider an example of something that is not a dense expression, for instance a diagonal matrix (a poor relation of the family, but a relation nevertheless). The
corresponding inheritance diagram is:

<pre>
EigenBase&lt;%DiagonalMatrix&gt;
  <-- DiagonalBase&lt;%DiagonalMatrix&gt;
    <-- DiagonalMatrix
</pre>


*/
}
